-- T-J3Z-06 / D-FH6-01 (quick task 260811-fh6) — WHO DISCHARGED THE ROW. GENERATED by `drizzle-kit
-- generate`, not hand-authored: a plain nullable text column is fully Drizzle-expressible, so it goes
-- through the generator (the schema.ts rule — only the GiST EXCLUDE stays hand-written), which also keeps
-- drizzle/meta/_journal.json and 0025_snapshot.json consistent for `npm run db:migrate`.
--
-- PURELY ADDITIVE AND BACKFILL-FREE. Nullable, no default, no constraint: every existing row reads
-- "discharger not recorded" BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by a separate decision not to run an UPDATE. The
-- 27 rows discharged on 2026-08-10 (runbook §4a) predate the column and keep NULL permanently — inventing
-- a discharger for a past act would be fabricating an audit record. That holds under RE-RESOLVE too:
-- `resolveAlert`'s `AND resolved_at IS NULL` guard excludes an already-discharged row, so a later `--by`
-- cannot retro-attribute it.
--
-- WHAT THE COLUMN IS FOR: `resolved_at` recorded THAT a money alert was discharged and nothing recorded BY
-- WHOM. On the QRPh rail a discharge asserts a human settled real held money that PayMongo cannot refund
-- through the API, so "which human" is the question a dispute actually asks.
--
-- THE CAVEAT, in one line because it travels with the column everywhere else too: this is an ASSERTED
-- identity, not an authenticated one — the operator CLI has no session, so the value is a claim, not proof.
--
-- NO FOREIGN KEY, deliberately (D4 one step harder): the value is a name typed at a CLI by someone who may
-- correspond to no `user` row at all. Provenance, not a join key. NO NEW INDEX either — nothing filters on
-- this column, and an unbounded-growth index belongs with the deferred retention decision
-- (src/lib/db/schema.ts), not bolted on here.
--
-- QUALIFICATION FORM, checked against the emitted SQL rather than assumed: the generator emitted the
-- UNQUALIFIED table name below. tests/helpers/db.ts:121 rewrites a literal `"public".` prefix to the
-- isolated test schema, so on this statement that rewrite is a harmless no-op and the ALTER lands in the
-- isolated schema via the `SET search_path TO "<schema>", public` that wraps every replayed statement.
-- The proof is the replay, not this reading: tests/ops/alerts.test.ts case 14 asserts the column against
-- `information_schema.columns` scoped to the replayed schema. A migration is NEVER verified with `tsc` —
-- the row type comes from schema.ts, so a declared-but-unmigrated column type-checks perfectly while every
-- integration test runs against a table without it (T-08-40; mutation M4 measures exactly that).
ALTER TABLE "audit" ADD COLUMN "resolved_by" text;
